s are athletes.\n\nPremise 3 says all Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities. So, any Oscar-nominated actor is a celebrity. Premise 5 says if a person is a celebrity, they are well paid. So, celebrities are well paid.\n\nPremise 6 says if a person is an athlete, they are famous. So, athletes are famous.\n\nPremise 7: If a person is famous and an athlete, then they live in a tax haven. So, famous AND athlete \u2192 tax haven.\n\nPremise 8: Djokovic is famous and an athlete. Therefore, according to premise 7, Djokovic must live in a tax haven.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward. Let me check again.\n\nFrom premise 2: Grand Slam champion is a professional tennis player. Professional tennis player is an athlete. So, Grand Slam champion is an athlete.\n\nPremise 3: Oscar-nominated actor is a celebrity. Premise 5: celebrity is well paid. Premise 6: athlete is famous. Premise 7: famous AND athlete \u2192 tax haven.\n\nTherefore, since Djokovic is an athlete (from premise 2), and he is famous (from premise 8), then he is famous AND athlete, so he must live in a tax haven (from premise 7). Therefore, Djokovic lives in a tax haven. So the answer should be A) True.\n\nBut wait, let me make sure there's no missing link. Let's go through each step again.\n\n1. Grand Slam champion is a professional tennis player (from 2). Professional tennis player is an athlete (from 4). Therefore, Grand Slam champion is an athlete.\n\n2. Oscar-nominated actor is a celebrity (from 3). Celebrity is well paid (from 5). Therefore, Oscar-nominated actors are well paid.\n\n3. All well-paid people live in tax havens (from 7). Therefore, if someone is well-paid, they live in a tax haven.\n\n4. Djokovic is an athlete (from 2). Therefore, he is well paid (from 3). Therefore, he is well paid and an athlete (from 6). Therefore, he must live in a tax haven (from 7). Therefore, Djokovic lives in a tax haven. So, the answer is A) True.\n\nBut wait, hold on. Let me check if there's any possible way this could