tecedent is true. So, in all cases, the antecedent is true. Therefore, the implication is only false if the antecedent is true and the consequent is false. But in all cases, the consequent is true. Therefore, the implication is always true. Therefore, the statement is true.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) True.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\\boxed{A}\n</think>\n\nGiven the context and the question, we need to determine if the statement \"If Rock is not both a turtle and a squirrel, then Rock is either cute or skittish\" is true, false, or uncertain.\n\n1. **Premises:**\n   - All rabbits are cute. (Rabbit \u2286 Cute)\n   - Some turtles exist. (Turtle exists, but not necessarily cute)\n   - An animal is either a rabbit or a squirrel. (Rabbit \u2228 Squirrel)\n   - All squirrels are skittish. (Squirrel \u2286 Skittish)\n   - Rock is still. (Rock \u2286 Still)\n\n2. **Statement Analysis:**\n   - The statement is an implication: (\u00acTurtle \u2227 \u00acSquirrel) \u2192 (Cute \u2228 Skittish)\n   - We need to check if this implication holds true.\n\n3. **Case Analysis:**\n   - **Case 1: Rock is a turtle.**\n     - Since all turtles are skittish, Rock is skittish.\n     - The antecedent (\u00acTurtle \u2227 \u00acSquirrel) is true.\n     - The consequent (Cute \u2228 Skittish) is true (Rock is skittish).\n     - The implication holds.\n\n   - **Case 2: Rock is a squirrel.**\n     - All squirrels are skittish, so Rock is skittish.\n     - The antecedent (\u00acTurtle \u2227 \u00acSquirrel) is true.\n     - The consequent (Cute \u2228 Skittish) is true (Rock is skittish).\n     - The implication holds.\n\n   - **Case 3: Rock is neither a turtle nor a squirrel.**\n     - The antecedent (\u00acTurtle \u2227 \u00acSquirrel) is true.\n     - The consequent (Cute \u2228 Skittish) is true (Rock is still, hence cute).\n     - The implication holds.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - In all cases, the antecedent (\u00acTurtle \u2227 \u00acSquirrel) is true, and the consequent (Cute \u2228 Skitt