      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out this logic problem. So, the context is about George and his ability to compose music. The question is whether the statement \"If George will not compose, then George can not compose\" is true, false, or uncertain. The options are A, B, or C. Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's parse the given information. The context gives three conditional statements:\n\n1. If George likes music, he wants to compose.\n2. If George has access to a program, he can compose.\n3. If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose.\n\nSo, translating these into logical statements:\n\n1. L \u2192 W (If George likes music, he wants to compose)\n2. A \u2192 C (If George has access to a program, he can compose)\n3. C \u2192 W (If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose)\n\nNow, the question is about the contrapositive of the third statement. The contrapositive of \"If P, then Q\" is \"If not Q, then not P\". So, the contrapositive of statement 3 would be: If George does not compose, then he does not have access to a program. Let me write that down:\n\n\u00acC \u2192 \u00acA\n\nSo, the question is asking whether the statement \"If George will not compose, George can not compose\" is true, false, or uncertain. In logical terms, this is equivalent to the contrapositive of the third statement. So, if \u00acC (George does not compose) implies \u00acA (he does not have access to a program), then the statement is true. If \u00acC does not imply \u00acA, then it's false. If it's not possible to determine whether \u00acC implies \u00acA, then it's uncertain.\n\nLet me check each part. First, let's recall the original statements:\n\n1. L \u2192 W: If George likes music, he wants to compose.\n2. A \u2192 C: If George has access to a program, he can compose.\n3. C \u2192 W: If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose.\n\nSo, from statement 3, we can infer that if he can compose and wants to compose, he will compose. But does that mean that if he can compose, he will compose? Let's see. If he can compose (C) and wants to compose (W), then he will compose (C \u2192 W). So, C \u2192 W is a stronger statement than C \u2192 W. Wait, actually, statement 3 is C \u2192 W, which is equivalent to (C \u2227 W) \u2192 W. So, if he can compose and wants to compose, he will compose. So, that's a stronger implication. But does that mean that if he can compose, he will compose? Let's see. If he can compose (C), then he can compose and wants to compose (C \u2227 W), so he will compose (C \u2192 W). So, C \u2192 W is a consequence of C. Therefore, if he can compose, he will compose. So, C \u2192 W is a valid implication.\n\nBut does that mean that if he can compose, he will compose? Let's think. Suppose George can compose. Then, he can compose and wants to compose (since he can compose and wants to compose). Therefore, by statement 3, he will compose. So, C \u2192 W is a valid implication. Therefore, if he can compose, he will compose. So, C \u2192 W is true.\n\nNow, the contrapositive of statement 3 is \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA. So, if George does not compose, then he does not have access to a program. Let's see if this is necessarily true. Is it possible for George to not have access to a program and still compose? Let's check the original statements.\n\nStatement 2 says that if he has access to a program, he can compose. So, A \u2192 C. Therefore, if he has access to a program, he can compose. But does that mean that if he can compose, he must have access to a program? Let's see. Suppose George can compose. Then, from statement 2, he must have access to a program. Therefore, C \u2192 A. So, if he can compose, he must have access to a program. Therefore, A \u2192 C is a valid implication. So, if he can compose, he has access to a program. Therefore, \u00acA \u2192 \u00acC is not necessarily true. Because if he can compose, he has access to a program, so \u00acA can't imply \u00acC. Because if he can compose, he has access to a program, so \u00acA is false, so \u00acA \u2192 \u00acC is automatically true. Wait, no. Wait, \u00acA is \"he does not have access to a program\". If he can compose, he has access to a program, so \u00acA is false. Therefore, \u00acA \u2192 \u00acC is a vacuously true statement because the antecedent is false. So, in logic, a statement of the form \"If P, then Q\" is considered true if P is false. So, \u00acA \u2192 \u00acC is true. Therefore, if George can compose, he has access to a program, so \u00acA is false, so \u00acA \u2192 \u00acC is true. Therefore, \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is not necessarily true. Because \u00acC is \"he does not have access to a program\". If he can compose, he has access to a program, so \u00acC is false. Therefore, \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is a true statement. Therefore, the contrapositive of statement 3 is true. Therefore, the statement \"If George will not compose, George can not compose\" is true. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, let me verify again. Let's take an example. Suppose George can compose. Then, from statement 2, he must have access to a program. So, he has access to a program. Therefore, \u00acC is false, so \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is true. Therefore, if George can compose, he has access to a program, so \u00acC is false, so \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is true. Therefore, \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is true. Therefore, the contrapositive is true. Therefore, the statement \"If George will not compose, George can not compose\" is true. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nBut let's check another example. Suppose George does not have access to a program. Then, from statement 2, he cannot compose. Therefore, \u00acC is true, so \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is false. Therefore, if George does not have access to a program, then he cannot compose. Therefore, the contrapositive is false. Therefore, the statement \"If George will not compose, George can not compose\" is false. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but in the first example, George can compose, so \u00acC is false, so \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is true. In the second example, George does not have access to a program, so \u00acC is true, so \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is false. Therefore, the statement \"If George will not compose, George can not compose\" is false. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut wait, the question is asking whether the statement \"If George will not compose, George can not compose\" is true, false, or uncertain. So, if George will not compose, does that mean George can not compose? Let's see. If George will not compose, that is, he does not compose. So, the antecedent is \"George will not compose\", which is equivalent to \"\u00acC\". The consequent is \"George can not compose\", which is \"\u00acA\". So, the statement is \"\u00acC \u2192 \u00acA\". As we saw, \u00acC \u2192 \u00acA is equivalent to \u00acA \u2192 \u00acC, which is true. Therefore, the statement is true. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nBut wait, let's check another angle. Let's consider the contrapositive. The contrapositive of \"If P, then Q\" is \"If not Q, then not P\". So, contrapositive of \"If C, then W\" is \"If \u00acW, then \u00acC\". So, the contrapositive is \"If George does not compose, then he does not have access to a program\". So, is this true? Let's see. If George does not compose, then he does not have access to a program. Is that necessarily true?\n\nFrom the original statements:\n\n1. L \u2192 W: If George likes music, he wants to compose.\n2. A \u2192 C: If George has access to a program, he can compose.\n3. C \u2192 W: If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose.\n\nSo, let's see. If George does not compose, then \u00acC. So, we need to see if \u00acC necessarily implies \u00acA. Is there a case where \u00acC is true but \u00acA is false? Let's see.\n\nSuppose George does not have access to a program. Then, from statement 2, he cannot compose. Th