y can't be spicy. But the second statement says all hotpots are spicy. So, if a hotpot is a Baked by Melissa product (since all Baked by Melissa products are cupcakes), then the hotpot is a spicy thing. But the first statement says no bakery is spicy. So, if a hotpot is a Baked by Melissa product (which is a bakery), then it can't be spicy. But the second statement says all hotpots are spicy. So, this is a contradiction. Therefore, there must be something wrong with my understanding.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the first statement is \"No bakery is spicy,\" which is a universal statement. So, all bakeries are not spicy. Then, the second statement is \"All hotpots are spicy,\" which is also a universal statement. But if a hotpot is a bakery, then it can't be spicy. But the second statement says all hotpots are spicy. Therefore, there's a contradiction. Therefore, the statements can't both be true. Therefore, the answer must be that the statement is false? But the options are A, B, or C. Wait, the options are A, B, or C. So, maybe the answer is B) False? But let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe I misread the fifth statement. Let me parse it again: \"If dried Thai chilies are spicy or a bakery, then dried Thai chilies are a hotpot or are spicy.\"\n\nSo, the structure is: If (dried Thai chilies are spicy OR dried Thai chilies are a bakery), then (dried Thai chilies are a hotpot OR spicy). So, let's break this down.\n\nFirst, let's note the premises:\n\n1. No bakery is spicy. (So, if X is a bakery, then X is not spicy.)\n2. All cupcakes are bakeries. (So, if X is a cupcake, then X is a bakery.)\n3. All hotpots are spicy. (So, if X is a hotpot, then X is spicy.)\n4. All products of Baked by Melissa are cupcakes. (So, if X is a Baked by Melissa product, then X is a cupcake.)\n\nNow, the fifth statement is a conditional: If (Dried Thai chilies are spicy OR Dried Thai chilies are a bakery), then (Dried Thai chilies are a hotpot OR spicy).\n\nSo, let's analyze this. Let's denote:\n\nLet P = Dried Thai chilies are spicy.\n\nLet Q = Dried Thai chilies are a bakery.\n\nLet R = Dried Thai chilies are a hotpot.\n\nThen, the fifth statement is: (P OR Q) \u2192 (R OR P).\n\nSo, the contrapositive would be: If not (R OR P), then not (P OR Q). But maybe that's not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let's see if we can derive any conclusions from the fifth statement.\n\nFirst, note that from premise 1: If X is a bakery, then X is not spicy. So, if Dried Thai chilies are a bakery, then they are not spicy. So, Q \u2192 \u00acP.\n\nBut from premise 3: All hotpots are spicy. So, R \u2192 P.\n\nSo, combining these two: Q \u2192 \u00acP and R \u2192 P. Therefore, Q \u2192 \u00acP and R \u2192 P. So, if Dried Thai chilies are a bakery, they are not spicy. If they are a hotpot, they are spicy.\n\nNow, the fifth statement is (P OR Q) \u2192 (R OR P). Let's see.\n\nSo, the antecedent is P OR Q. The consequent is R OR P.\n\nWe need to check if whenever P OR Q is true, then R OR P is true.\n\nBut let's see. Let's consider cases.\n\nCase 1: P is true (Dried Thai chilies are spicy). Then, since P is true, the consequent R OR P is true. So, in this case, the implication holds.\n\nCase 2: Q is true (Dried Thai chilies are a bakery). Then, since Q is true, the antecedent P OR Q is true (because Q is true). Then, the consequent R OR P is true. So, the implication holds here as well.\n\nCase 3: Both P and Q are true. Then, the antecedent P OR Q is true. The consequent R OR P is true. So, the implication holds here as well.\n\nCase 4: P is false (Dried Thai chilies are not spicy) and Q is false (Dried Thai chilies are not a bakery). Then, the antecedent P OR Q is false. The consequent R OR P is false. So, the implication is vacuously true.\n\nCase 5: P is false and Q is true. Then, the antecedent P OR Q is true (since Q is true). The consequent R OR P is true (since R is true). So, the implication holds.\n\nCase 6: P is true and Q is false. Then, the antecedent P OR Q 