      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out this logic problem. So, first, let me parse the given information step by step. \n\nThe context says: \"No one nice to animals is mean to animals.\" So, if someone is nice to animals, they can't be mean to them. In logical terms, that would be \u00acN \u2192 \u00acM, where N is \"nice to animals\" and M is \"mean to animals.\" So, if someone is nice, they can't be mean. Got that.\n\nNext, \"Some grumpy people are mean to animals.\" Hmm, \"some\" here probably means a subset. So, there exists at least one grumpy person who is mean to animals. Let me note that as G \u2229 M \u2260 \u2205. So, there's at least one grumpy person who is mean.\n\nThen, \"All animal lovers are nice to animals.\" So, if someone loves animals, they must be nice to them. That translates to L \u2192 N, where L is \"loves animals\" and N is \"nice to animals.\"\n\nAlso, \"If someone owns a pet, then they love animals.\" So, O \u2192 L, where O is \"owns a pet.\" \n\nAnd finally, \"Tom is a pet owner.\" So, T is an O, meaning Tom owns a pet.\n\nThe question is: Is the statement \"Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals\" true, false, or uncertain? The options are A, B, or C.\n\nFirst, let's break down the statement: \"Tom is not a grumpy person and mean to animals.\" In logical terms, that would be \u00acG \u2227 M. So, Tom is not grumpy (\u00acG) and he is mean to animals (M). \n\nWe need to check if this is necessarily true, or if there's some ambiguity or if it's possible but not certain.\n\nLet's analyze each part:\n\n1. \u00acG: Tom is not a grumpy person. So, he could be a nice person, a grumpy person, or a grumpy person who is nice. But the statement is combining this with M.\n\n2. M: Tom is mean to animals. So, he is mean, regardless of his grumpiness.\n\nSo, the question is: If Tom is not a grumpy person (\u00acG), does that necessarily mean he is mean to animals (M)?\n\nLooking at the premises:\n\n- All animal lovers are nice to animals (L \u2192 N). So, if someone loves animals, they are nice. But Tom might not love animals. However, if Tom is a pet owner (O), then he loves animals (L). So, if Tom is a pet owner, he must be a nice person (N). Therefore, if Tom is a pet owner, he is nice. But he might not be a pet owner. Wait, but the premises don't say anything about people who aren't pet owners. So, Tom could be a pet owner or not. If he is a pet owner, he is nice. If he isn't a pet owner, he could be a nice person or a grumpy person. But the statement is about Tom being not grumpy and mean. So, even if he isn't a pet owner, he could still be a nice person (so not grumpy) and mean to animals. But wait, if he is a nice person, he can't be mean. So, if he is a nice person, he can't be mean. Therefore, if Tom is not a grumpy person (\u00acG), then he must be nice (N). But if he is nice, he can't be mean. So, if he is not a grumpy person, he can't be mean. Therefore, if Tom is not a grumpy person, he must be nice. But he could be a pet owner or not. However, if he is a pet owner, he is nice. If he isn't a pet owner, he could be a nice person or a grumpy person. But the statement is about Tom being not grumpy and mean. So, even if he isn't a pet owner, he could still be a nice person (so not grumpy) and mean. But wait, if he is a nice person, he can't be mean. So, if he is not a grumpy person, he must be nice. Therefore, if Tom is not a grumpy person, he must be nice. But he could be a pet owner or not. However, if he is a pet owner, he is nice. If he isn't a pet owner, he could be a nice person or a grumpy person. But the statement is about Tom being not grumpy and mean. So, even if he isn't a pet owner, he could still be a nice person (so not grumpy) and mean. But wait, if he is a nice person, he can't be mean. So, if he is not a grumpy person, he must be nice. Therefore, if Tom is not a grumpy person, he must be nice. But he could be a pet owner or not. However, if he is a pet owner, he