d, he can't be an alligator. But Ted is an aligator. Therefore, this is a contradiction. Therefore, Ted cannot be both a pet and a cow. So, the answer would be B) False.\n\nBut let me check again. Maybe I made a mistake here. Let's go through it step by step.\n\n1. Ted is an aligator. (Given)\n2. Ted is a pet. (Given)\n3. All cows are bovines. So, if Ted is a cow, he is a bovine. (From 1)\n4. Some pets are cows. So, there exists at least one pet that is a cow. (From 2)\n5. If something is a bovine, then it is domesticated. So, if Ted is a bovine (from 3), then he is domesticated. (From 4)\n6. No domesticated animals are alligators. So, if Ted is domesticated (from 5), he cannot be an alligator. (From 4)\n7. Ted is an aligator. (Given)\n\nSo, if Ted is a pet (2), then he is a cow (1). Then, being a cow, he is a bovine (1). Then, being a bovine, he is domesticated (4). But statement 4 says domesticated animals are not alligators. Therefore, if Ted is domesticated, he can't be an alligator. But Ted is an aligator. Therefore, this is a contradiction. Therefore, Ted cannot be both a pet and a cow. Therefore, the statement \"Ted is a pet and a cow\" is false. So, the answer is B.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's another way. Maybe Ted is a pet but not a cow? But according to statement 2, if Ted is a pet, he must be a cow. So, that's impossible. Therefore, Ted must be a cow, which is a pet. Therefore, he can't be a pet and a cow. Therefore, the answer is B) False.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Ted is a pet but not a cow? But statement 2 says \"Some pets are cows,\" so there exists at least one pet that is a cow. If Ted is a pet, he must be a cow. Therefore, he can't be a pet and a cow. Therefore, the answer is B.\n\nAlternatively, maybe Ted is a pet but not a cow? But statement 2 says \"Some pets are cows,\" so there must be at least one pet that is a cow. Therefore, if Ted is a pet, he must be a cow. Therefore, he can't be a pet and a cow. Therefore, the answer is B.\n\nAlt