ther state. But that doesn't affect the statement about Missoula. So, the only thing we need to know is whether Montana includes Missoula. Since the context says Montana includes Butte, Helena, and Missoula, then yes, Montana is home to Missoula. Therefore, the answer is A) True.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any possible misinterpretation. For example, could Montana be a country? No, the context is about cities in Montana, so it's a state in the US. So, Billings is a city in Montana, which is a state that includes Butte, Helena, and Missoula. Therefore, Missoula is in Montana. So, the answer is True. I think that's solid. So, option A is correct.\n</think>\n\nThe context states that Montana includes the cities of Butte, Helena, and Missoula. Billings is a city in Montana, which means Montana is home to all three cities. Therefore, the statement \" Montana is home to the city of Missoula\" is true. \n\n**Answer:** \\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n**Note:** The problem mentions Pierre, a city not in Montana, but this does not affect the determination of whether Montana is home to Missoula. The key information is that Montana includes the specified cities. Since Missoula is one of those cities, it is indeed in Montana. Thus, the answer is True.  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n**Final Answer:** \\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\boxed{A} (True)  \n\n\\bo