h at home, then they are working RH. So, premise 4 is LH \u2192 RH.\n\nPremise 5 is that all employees in other countries work RH. So, if an employee is in other countries, then RH.\n\nPremise 6 is that no managers work RH. So, if an employee is a manager, then RH.\n\nPremise 3 is that employees will either have lunch in the company or have lunch at home. So, every employee is in the OR of LH and RH.\n\nPremise 7 is that James is either a manager and present today, or not a manager and present today.\n\nSo, James's status is a combination of being a manager and being present today.\n\nNow, the statement is: If James is either a manager or in other countries, then James either has lunch at home and works remotely, or neither has lunch at home nor works remotely.\n\nSo, the antecedent is J is a manager OR J is in other countries. The consequent is J has lunch at home and works RH OR J doesn't have lunch at home and doesn't work RH.\n\nWe need to check if whenever J is in the OR of being a manager or in other countries, then J must be in the OR of LH and RH.\n\nBut according to premise 7, James is either a manager and present today, or not a manager and present today. So, his status is a combination of being a manager and being present today.\n\nPremise 4 says that if an employee has lunch at home, then they are working RH. So, LH \u2192 RH.\n\nPremise 5 says that all employees in other countries work RH. So, if an employee is in other countries, then RH.\n\nPremise 6 says that no managers work RH. So, if an employee is a manager, then RH.\n\nPremise 3 says that every employee is in the OR of LH and RH.\n\nSo, let's analyze the antecedent: J is a manager OR J is in other countries.\n\nCase 1: J is a manager and present today.\n\nThen, in the OR of being a manager and present today, we need to check if Q holds. Q is (LH \u2227 RH) \u2228 (~LH \u2227 ~RH). So, Q is true if either he has lunch at home and works RH, or he doesn't have lunch at home and doesn't work RH.\n\n