      "text": "<think>\nOkay, let's try to figure out this logic problem step by step. So, first, let me parse the given information and see how the different conditions connect.\n\nThe context gives several conditional statements:\n\n1. If people spill a lot of food on their clothing, then they do not tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning. (So, S \u2192 \u00acT and \u00acC)\n2. All foodies who go out frequently to find new food restaurants to try are people who spill a lot of food on their clothing. (F \u2192 S)\n3. If people grew up as only children with no siblings, then they tidy their spaces consistently and enjoy the process of cleaning. (G \u2192 T and C)\n4. If people have parents who are very busy with their regular jobs, then they grew up as only children with no siblings. (P \u2192 G)\n5. All households with a high net income have people who have parents who are very busy with their regular jobs. (H \u2192 P)\n6. Peter is either both a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing and grew up as an only child with no siblings, or he is neither a person who spills a lot of food on his clothing nor grew up as an only child with no siblings. (P \u2192 (S \u2227 G) \u2228 \u00ac(S \u2227 G))\n\nThe question is: Is the statement \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and belong to a household with a high net income or he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and does not belong to a household with a high net income.\" true, false, or uncertain?\n\nSo, translating the question into logical terms: Is Peter a foodie (F) who either goes out frequently (S) and is in a high net income household (H), or he is a foodie who goes out frequently but is not in a high net income household (F \u2227 S \u2227 \u00acH)?\n\nWait, let me parse that again. The statement is: \"Peter is either a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and belong to a household with a high net income or he is a foodie who goes out frequently to find new food restaurants to try and does not belong to a household with a high net income.\"\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n1. Either:\n   a. Peter is a foodie (F) and he is in a high net income household (H), or\n   b. Peter is a foodie (F) and he is not in a high net income household (\u00acH)\n\nSo, the statement is saying that Peter is either in both H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the logical structure is:\n\nPeter is F \u2227 (H \u2228 \u00acH)\n\nBut wait, \u00acH is equivalent to H \u2228 \u00acH. So, Peter is F \u2227 (H \u2228 \u00acH). Which is the same as Peter being F and either H or \u00acH. So, Peter is F and (H or \u00acH). So, Peter is F and (H or \u00acH). So, that would mean Peter is F and H, or Peter is F and \u00acH. So, the statement is saying that Peter is either in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is saying that Peter is in H and F, or in F but not in H. So, the statement is say