. So, the answer should be B) False.\n\nBut let me check again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\n1. Harry is a gentleman (statement 6). Therefore, he is in the gentleman set.\n\n2. From statement 4: gentlemen are a subset of handsome people. Therefore, he is handsome.\n\n3. From statement 3: all evil people are ugly. Therefore, he cannot be evil. So, he is not evil.\n\n4. From statement 2: no ugly person is handsome. Therefore, he is not ugly.\n\n5. From statement 5: all CEOs are gentlemen. Therefore, if he is a CEO, he must be a gentleman. But he is a gentleman, so he can't be a CEO. Therefore, he is not a CEO.\n\nTherefore, Harry is a gentleman, not a CEO. Therefore, the statement \"Harry is a CEO\" is false. So, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut wait, let me check if there's any other possible way. Suppose there's a person who is a gentleman but not a CEO. But according to statement 5, all CEOs are gentlemen. So, if Harry is a CEO, he must be a gentleman. Therefore, he can't be a CEO unless he's a gentleman. But he is a gentleman, so he can't be a CEO. Therefore, the only possibility is that Harry is a gentleman, not a CEO. Therefore, the statement is false. So, answer B.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a trick here. Let me see.\n\nWait, the problem is in Chinese, but the translation seems okay. Let me check again.\n\nGiven:\n\n1. A man is either kind or evil. So, K or E, no overlap.\n\n2. No ugly person is handsome. So, U \u2260 H.\n\n3. All evil people are ugly. So, E \u2286 U.\n\n4. All gentlemen are handsome. So, G \u2286 H.\n\n5. All CEOs are gentlemen. So, C \u2286 G.\n\n6. Harry is a gentleman. So, H is in G.\n\nFrom 4: H is in H, which is a subset of H. So, H is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H is in G, which is in H. So, H